WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:06.230
China “appears to be… construct[ing] the
ultimate tool of social control” … a “comprehensive

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:09.870
‘credit score’ system” … that will
“make your head spin.”

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:19.000
…That’s what the American Civil Liberties
Union (ACLU) wrote on October 5th, 2015.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.460
This newly emerging, soon to be all-encompassing
surveillance network, it said, would give

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:30.340
everyone in China a score between 350 and
nine hundred and fifty.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:35.590
But unlike, say, American credit scores, these
would punish everything from jaywalking to

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:38.079
government criticism.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:44.410
Even more disturbingly, you could be responsible
for the actions of friends and family.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:48.100
The rest of this article mainly focused on
the United States.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.059
The ACLU, after all, is an American non-profit.

00:00:51.059 --> 00:00:57.769
It lobbies for things like free speech, privacy,
and criminal justice reform.

00:00:57.769 --> 00:01:00.170
This was not new reporting.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:06.380
The author was open about his sources — other
articles he had read online.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:10.479
Neither did he claim any expertise on China.

00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:15.520
In fact, just three days later, he updated
the article, softening the tone and emphasizing

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.890
that he only meant to caution against “potential
abuses” in America.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.530
But it didn’t matter.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:28.340
What mattered was that a high-profile, well-respected
institution had stamped its seal of approval

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.920
and other organizations took notice.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:38.060
Two days after the original ACLU article went
live and a day before it issued the update,

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:43.420
Computerworld re-published it, calling the
Chinese system “Orwellian”.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.830
Popular Science wrote that although, quote,
“the system isn’t explicitly dystopian

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:48.830
yet…

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:52.310
it has all the pieces to be dystopian at a
moment’s notice.”

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:58.680
And Reason claimed these scores would soon
“define Chinese citizens' lives”.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:03.840
In less than a week, the Social Credit System
had not only entered the English-media landscape,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.050
but also, seemingly, amassed a body of evidence.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:12.640
Multiple sources endorsing the same alarming
conclusion.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.010
Never mind that they all referenced the same
ACLU report written by a non-expert, largely

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:23.520
about the United States, itself based on earlier
speculation.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.349
News travels fast.

00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:27.530
And headlines even faster.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:33.980
There was no need for some grand conspiracy
to smear China or bad faith on the part of

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.980
any individual author.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:41.590
The simple market pressures of 21st-century
journalism were enough to make a story appear

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.290
out of thin air.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:49.000
America’s (often quite justified) suspicion
of the Chinese government and technology in

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.780
general, were just icing on the cake.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.580
Since then, the social credit system has truly
taken on a life of its own, with coverage

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:03.150
by the Financial Times, Economist, Fortune,
and countless others.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:08.120
This now eight year-old game of telephone
culminated in an appearance at the White House,

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:13.200
with Vice President Mike Pence describing
it as “an Orwellian system premised on controlling

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.370
virtually every facet of human life.”

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:22.129
And yet, ask a friend, colleague, or classmate
who’s spent time in China about their experience

00:03:22.129 --> 00:03:25.500
and you’ll likely be met with confusion.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.280
This is not a case of censorship.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.500
The Chinese government does engage in extensive
censorship, but for the social credit system

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:38.860
to work as described, the public must be,
at a minimum, aware of its existence.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:42.060
Neither is this the result of propaganda.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:46.860
The government does engage in propaganda,
but in this case, the response is not anger,

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.620
frustration, or disagreement — just confusion.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:56.489
Because, for all its well-documented, genuine
atrocities, the “Orwellian” credit score

00:03:56.489 --> 00:04:01.640
system imagined by many in the media simply
doesn’t exist.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:06.489
The truth, however, is far more revealing.

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:07.489
Sponsored by Skillshare.

00:04:07.489 --> 00:04:14.610
Get a one-month free trial and start learning
everything from programming to graphic design.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:20.470
In June 2014, the State Council of China published
this “Planning Outline for the Establishment

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.440
of a Social Credit System”

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:27.070
Now, the first thing you should know about
this document is that it’s incredibly boring

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:30.199
and almost artfully vague.

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:34.500
Full of dense phrases like “Promote the
improvement of legal person governance”,

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:40.980
you almost have to admire how little it manages
to say in 16,000 Chinese characters.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.850
The second thing to note is the broader context
in which it was released.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:50.790
One month earlier, in May of 2014, the same
government announced what it described as

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:55.759
a “war on terror” against violent extremism
in Xinjiang.

00:04:55.759 --> 00:05:00.410
This would later evolve into a full-blown
attack on Uyghurs and other ethnic minorities

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:05.810
including mass incarceration, surveillance,
and violence that continues, in modified form,

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:08.100
to this day.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.279
In other words, this was a time when much
of the world was becoming — understandably

00:05:12.279 --> 00:05:17.470
— increasingly skeptical of the Chinese
government, and here was this new, suspiciously

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:21.250
ambiguous notion of “social credit”.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:26.600
The motive for some kind of inescapable system
of surveillance and control seemed undeniable.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:32.400
In fact, what made the myth so potent was
that it did contain an element of truth.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:38.150
In the years since, China has constructed
a similarly oppressive, albeit less high-tech,

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:44.870
police state in Xinjiang, involving DNA collection,
phone searches, and security checkpoints.

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:48.810
Besides, what else could the term “social
credit” refer to?

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:55.039
And if it truly had nothing to hide, why was
the plan written in such cryptic language?

00:05:55.039 --> 00:06:00.729
But what few casual observers understood is
that cryptic, inscrutable Leninist-steeped

00:06:00.729 --> 00:06:04.800
jargon is kind of the Chinese Communist Party’s
whole thing.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.600
And there’s a very specific reason…

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:15.600
The benefits of autocracy for an autocrat
are so obvious — complete, unchecked authority

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.729
— that it’s easy to forget the trade-off.

00:06:18.729 --> 00:06:21.900
With complete authority comes complete responsibility.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.009
There’s no one to blame.

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:29.910
Consider how often Democrats and Republicans
use each other as scapegoats.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:32.470
Dictators don’t have that luxury.

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:37.590
So, to insulate themselves from criticism,
they often delegate a lot more power than

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:39.190
you might expect.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:46.240
Now, make no mistake, Beijing always gets
the final word, but think of it as an architect.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:51.650
It produces the national blueprint, first
in the form of a policy document, and later,

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:57.370
a policy outline, but whose actual implementation
it outsources to lower levels of government

00:06:57.370 --> 00:07:00.129
— its engineers.

00:07:00.129 --> 00:07:04.289
When the actual building looks nothing like
the concept art, the central government can

00:07:04.289 --> 00:07:10.729
keep its hands clean by pointing to its beautifully
vague, boringly-unobjectionable sketches.

00:07:10.729 --> 00:07:15.560
At the same time, it also, of course, has
things it wants to accomplish — things that

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:17.940
need to be communicated to the ‘engineers’.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:24.040
Thus, while these policy documents, as we
saw, are indeed horribly tedious, they’re

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:26.340
not entirely devoid of content.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.630
You just have to understand Party-speak.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:34.260
So, what did it mean by “social credit”?

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:39.389
According to Jeremy Daum, Senior Fellow at
Yale Law School’s Paul Tsai China Center

00:07:39.389 --> 00:07:43.759
and someone fluent in Party-speak, it was
never a single policy.

00:07:43.759 --> 00:07:47.240
It wasn’t even a very well-outlined goal.

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:53.300
Instead, it was always, quote, “shorthand
for a broad range of efforts”.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:55.780
The first of which was financial.

00:07:55.780 --> 00:08:02.509
In short, the millions born before the One-Child
Policy became law in 1980 were in their prime

00:08:02.509 --> 00:08:08.949
working-age years during the Chinese boom
of the 80s, 90s, and early 2000s.

00:08:08.949 --> 00:08:14.550
That large mass of workers kept labor costs
down, attracting foreign companies, who, in

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:17.850
turn, invested that profit back into the country.

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:23.880
But, as all those workers began to retire,
Chinese labor became a lot more expensive,

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:28.870
leading to lower profits, and therefore, less
investment.

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:33.529
That investment, however, could be replaced
with consumption.

00:08:33.529 --> 00:08:38.539
In summary: if China was to keep growing,
it needed to start spending.

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:44.610
Yet, at the time, just one-fifth of the population
had a credit report.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:48.480
Without banks or credit cards, they couldn’t
take out loans.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:51.810
And without loans, they couldn’t spend as
much money.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:58.200
Meanwhile, much of this large, unbanked population
used Chinese apps like WeChat and AliPay on

00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.970
a daily basis.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:05.339
So, the thinking was, what if all that data
could be used to create alternative credit

00:09:05.339 --> 00:09:06.600
scores?

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:12.660
Eight private companies were selected to do
just that as part of a pilot project.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:18.000
The most famous was “Sesame Credit”, an
optional service that gave users a three-digit

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.090
score.

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:25.060
The second and larger focus of “social credit”
was centered around trust.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:30.690
Ironically, while outsiders view China as
a tightly-controlled, closely-monitored police

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:37.399
state, many in China saw their country as
the “Wild West” — untamed, unregulated,

00:09:37.399 --> 00:09:38.959
and lacking in consequences.

00:09:38.959 --> 00:09:45.440
In 2008, for instance, a company was caught
using toxic chemicals in its milk and baby

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.300
formula, making thousands of children sick.

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:53.500
Scandals like these left deep nationwide scars.

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:59.579
Some parents are — understandably — still
suspicious of Chinese milk today.

00:09:59.579 --> 00:10:04.670
At the same time, corruption — once tolerated
as a necessary ingredient to rapid economic

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:08.580
growth — was beginning to reach a boiling
point.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:13.089
Not only was the Party losing control of its
own members, but the public was starting to

00:10:13.089 --> 00:10:14.470
take notice.

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:20.651
Stories of county bureaucrats driving Lamborghinis,
despite their measly official salaries, were

00:10:20.651 --> 00:10:23.420
attracting a bit too much attention.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:28.500
And, in the eyes of the Party, all these things
were connected.

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:30.380
The milk, the corruption…

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:34.590
it all represented a crisis of moral failure.

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:40.470
One consequence of which was a general lack
of trust throughout Chinese society.

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:47.310
The solution was to build what it called,
in its typically ambiguous way, a “reputation

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:48.310
society”.

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:53.610
Remember, this was the architect telling the
engineer: “here’s a vaguely-defined problem,

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:56.350
now go try and solve it.”

00:10:56.350 --> 00:11:00.740
The results were as random and chaotic as
you’d expect.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:06.329
What, as a local bureaucrat, are you supposed
to do with those instructions?

00:11:06.329 --> 00:11:09.770
No one knows, and that’s the point.

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:14.910
A few dozen cities across China rushed to
implement their own “social credit” reputation

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:19.660
systems, each with their own set of rules
and rewards.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:24.910
One city announced it would subtract 3 points
for every five cigarettes smoked and add 5

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:28.350
for every 15,000 steps walked.

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:33.089
No explanation was offered for how these numbers
were determined, much less how this could

00:11:33.089 --> 00:11:37.160
possibly be enforced, but it didn’t matter.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:41.650
The public backlash was so strong that the
proposal was abandoned.

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:47.790
Bizarrely, another city said it would penalize
drunk driving and reward volunteer work, but

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:50.360
then decided to make the system optional.

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:56.639
A third city gave every citizen a grade from
A to D, until someone compared it to the Japanese

00:11:56.639 --> 00:11:59.990
Occupation of China and it quietly disappeared.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:05.250
It was all very weird and impractical.

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:09.680
Needless to say, the Central government was…
thoroughly unimpressed.

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:13.779
Evidently, the construction didn’t match
the blueprint.

00:12:13.779 --> 00:12:19.430
Alternatively, perhaps Beijing miscalculated
the public’s reaction and saw its opportunity

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:25.340
to swoop in and ‘protect’ the country
from those terrible, no-good engineers.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:30.160
The People’s Bank of China refused to renew
the licenses of all eight companies participating

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:33.209
in the financial pilot project.

00:12:33.209 --> 00:12:38.240
Beijing also called out several local governments
for being overzealous, instructing them to

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:42.490
only punish what was already illegal.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:47.320
The third and final component of social credit
are blacklists.

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:52.910
Previously, each government ministry maintained
separate records, meaning someone could violate

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:57.579
the rules of, say, the tax authorities, without
the food inspector knowing.

00:12:57.579 --> 00:13:03.560
Now they’re supposed to share that data,
not only internally but also with the public,

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:09.500
so, for example, you could search the name
of a company and decide if it’s reputable.

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:14.260
Generally trustworthy entities are placed
on so-called “red-lists” and repeat offenders

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:16.910
on blacklists.

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:22.600
Most of these are companies, though their
owners and managers can be implicated too.

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:29.440
As a ballpark, somewhere between about 1 and
2% of companies and 0.15 to .3 percent of

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:32.050
people are blacklisted each year.

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:37.529
In the most extreme case, offenders are banned
from taking flights or trains, or sending

00:13:37.529 --> 00:13:39.810
their children to private schools.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:45.720
But these restrictions only apply to one type
of offense, those who refuse to pay damages

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:49.250
found by a judge, despite having the requisite
funds.

00:13:49.250 --> 00:13:54.480
Once they’ve fulfilled their court orders,
they’re removed from the blacklist.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:14:00.509
By 2018, over 12 million of these “judgment
defaulters” had been affected.

00:14:00.509 --> 00:14:04.180
Some cities even broadcast their names in
movie theaters.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:08.259
Okay, let’s review…

00:14:08.259 --> 00:14:13.610
One part of “social credit” was “financial”,
one about cities incentivizing “good behavior”,

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:16.930
and the third an attempt to share government
data.

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:22.279
The first involved eight companies, the second,
dozens of different cities, and the third,

00:14:22.279 --> 00:14:25.149
several independent ministries.

00:14:25.149 --> 00:14:28.040
One was private, the other two, public.

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:30.600
Some were optional, others not.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:35.320
Together, they have very little in common
besides the label “social credit”.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:40.820
If you’re not at least slightly confused
by now, you should probably be concerned.

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:45.370
Remember, this was not designed for global
comprehension.

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:49.050
Actually, it wasn’t really designed for
local comprehension.

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:54.540
It was designed to leave the door open for
experimentation and to retain some plausible

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:57.200
deniability for the central government.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:03.800
So, it should be no surprise that virtually
every detail was lost on the media.

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:09.750
Instead of three separate projects, many journalists
lumped them all together as one.

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:14.690
The inputs of privately-owned Sesame Credit
— your shopping behavior, for instance — were

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:19.870
paired with the state-sponsored punishments
of the entirely separate blacklists — travel

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:21.480
restrictions, for example.

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:28.170
Thus, the story became “if you buy the wrong
things, the CCP will ban you from travel”.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:33.800
“Social credit” was an easy device through
which to conceive of and critique Chinese

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:34.860
authoritarianism.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:41.370
The irony being that the reason this 1984-style
surveillance was so believable in the first

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:45.610
place is also the reason the Party doesn’t
need it.

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:49.480
Chinese surveillance isn’t some new development.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:54.660
The battle for anonymity was lost well before
the birth of social credit.

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:59.149
All accounts on all Chinese websites require
real-name registration.

00:15:59.149 --> 00:16:02.380
So does every SIM card, and internet cafe.

00:16:02.380 --> 00:16:07.511
If and when laws — including social credit
— are weaponized, it’s because of the

00:16:07.511 --> 00:16:12.990
underlying system — the nearly unchecked
discretion afforded to individual police officers

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:14.870
and bureaucrats.

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:20.269
Now, it would be clean and simple to end the
video here by concluding that journalists

00:16:20.269 --> 00:16:22.970
are just ‘lazy’ and ran with a false narrative.

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:26.760
But that too would be lazy.

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:30.910
This is not just a case of journalists seeing
what they wanted to see.

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:36.470
Because China also feeds into these pre-existing
anxieties.

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:40.380
To see how, humor me with one final tangent.

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:46.810
In democracies, ‘politics’ seem to get
in the way of simple solutions to healthcare,

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:49.709
education, and infrastructure.

00:16:49.709 --> 00:16:53.759
When Congress can’t seem to get anything
done, it’s easy to start thinking of party

00:16:53.759 --> 00:16:57.090
politics as a bug, not a feature.

00:16:57.090 --> 00:17:03.660
“If only I were General Secretary for a
day, everything would be so much better”

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:11.750
If democracy is messy, inefficient, and complicated,
autocracy must be clean, efficient, and simple.

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:14.589
But not necessarily.

00:17:14.589 --> 00:17:18.439
If you’re a student, think about the last
time you tried to herd the sheep that were

00:17:18.439 --> 00:17:21.169
the members of your group project.

00:17:21.169 --> 00:17:27.210
If you work in an office, recall how difficult
it is to get sales and finance to collaborate.

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:33.320
Now multiply that complexity by 100 million
people — the size of the Communist Party.

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:38.220
Though they may march under the same banner,
people are people.

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:45.450
100 million members means 100 million feuds,
allegiances, and career ambitions.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:50.700
By delegating power in order to share responsibility,
China opens the door to a certain version

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:53.350
of messy politics.

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:58.299
Both the local governments and private companies
responsible for implementing Beijing’s vision

00:17:58.299 --> 00:18:05.030
of “social credit” had their own incentives,
one of which was to overstate their capabilities.

00:18:05.030 --> 00:18:10.150
If you think Silicon Valley is the capital
of empty buzzwords, try talking to executives

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:15.710
at Alibaba or Party officials from up-and-coming
cities like Suzhou.

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:20.850
Desperate to impress their bosses in Beijing,
they throw around terms like “big data”,

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:25.289
“blockchain”, and “smart cities” like
it’s no one’s business.

00:18:25.289 --> 00:18:28.179
Take Sesame Credit, for instance.

00:18:28.179 --> 00:18:32.720
To curry favor with the authorities, the company
painted a beautiful picture full of super

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:35.360
advanced algorithms.

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:41.429
To entice users, it advertised powerful benefits
like travel visas to Europe.

00:18:41.429 --> 00:18:46.309
The English-language media, already believing
Sesame Credit to be the official manifestation

00:18:46.309 --> 00:18:50.980
of “social credit”, effectively bought
into this marketing, which it then resold

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:57.150
to a global audience by emphasizing how this
“super advanced” technology could be weaponized.

00:18:57.150 --> 00:19:02.030
In reality, Sesame Credit was a — frankly
amateur — version of the Target RedCard

00:19:02.030 --> 00:19:07.580
— it calculated how often, not what you
purchased, whether you’d completed your

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:13.049
profile, not what it said, and how many accounts
you’d added on the app as “friends”,

00:19:13.049 --> 00:19:14.980
not who they were.

00:19:14.980 --> 00:19:19.120
In exchange for sharing this basic data with
the company, you could do things like rent

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:21.330
a bike without leaving a deposit.

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:27.010
A high score couldn’t grant you a visa to
Europe — only Europe can do that.

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:32.909
All it could do is help prove your finances,
replacing one of several documents required

00:19:32.909 --> 00:19:34.789
to apply.

00:19:34.789 --> 00:19:40.190
Likewise, local governments love nebulous
terms like “social credit” because unlike,

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:44.539
say, GDP growth, it’s very hard to measure.

00:19:44.539 --> 00:19:49.789
This lack of oversight makes it all too easy
to exaggerate claims about “algorithms”

00:19:49.789 --> 00:19:54.420
— which often meant simple addition and
subtraction — and “big data” — which

00:19:54.420 --> 00:20:00.500
was often recorded on spreadsheets, or in
one city, I kid you not, clipboards.

00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:05.549
This, for instance, is the average number
of data points per citizen in each of these

00:20:05.549 --> 00:20:08.870
five pilot cities.

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:14.429
The social credit dystopia imagined by many,
on the other hand, would require close coordination

00:20:14.429 --> 00:20:19.809
between hundreds of different ministries — all
of which are competing for promotions, budget

00:20:19.809 --> 00:20:23.200
allocations, and regulatory authority.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:28.730
This is not at all to say China is incapable
of doing big and scary things.

00:20:28.730 --> 00:20:32.830
The point is that we should approach its claims
about “big data” and “machine learning”

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:37.740
with the same degree of skepticism as we would
any Silicon Valley startup.

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:43.929
Currently, one side is primed to see China
as ten-feet tall and the other is incentivized

00:20:43.929 --> 00:20:48.390
to exaggerate its height — what could go
wrong?

00:20:48.390 --> 00:20:53.690
